---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import GuideLayout from "../../components/GuideLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";

const test = `import gleam/json
import gleam/option.{Some}
import gleeunit/should

import watershed/sluice_js
import watershed

pub fn two_teammates_converge_test() {
  let sluice = sluice_js.start(tenant: "flow-co", document: "flowboard")
  let doc_a = sluice_js.connect(sluice, "ada")
  let doc_b = sluice_js.connect(sluice, "boaz")
  sluice_js.settle(sluice)              // complete both handshakes

  let board_a = watershed.root(doc_a)
  let board_b = watershed.root(doc_b)

  // Concurrent card moves, including a same-card race.
  watershed.set(board_a, "card-7", json.string("Doing"))
  watershed.set(board_b, "card-9", json.string("Review"))
  watershed.set(board_a, "card-4", json.string("Doing"))
  watershed.set(board_b, "card-4", json.string("Done"))
  sluice_js.settle(sluice)              // deliver every edit everywhere

  // Uncontested writes land on both replicas.
  watershed.get(board_b, "card-7")
  |> should.equal(Some(json.string("Doing")))

  // Both teammates agree on the contested card — deterministically,
  // no polling, no server, no timing accident.
  watershed.get(board_a, "card-4")
  |> should.equal(watershed.get(board_b, "card-4"))
}`;

const race = `// Script the race instead of hoping for a schedule: deliver frames on demand.
watershed.set(watershed.root(doc_a), "card-4", json.string("Doing"))
watershed.set(watershed.root(doc_b), "card-4", json.string("Done"))

sluice_js.step(sluice)     // deliver exactly one frame
sluice_js.settle(sluice)   // then drain the rest to quiescence`;
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="testing"
  description="Step six of the watershed build guide: use the in-memory sluice sequencer to run deterministic multi-client convergence tests with explicit delivery and no external server."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Prove convergence without a server</h2>
    <p>
      A multiplayer application must produce the same state on each client.
      Developers often use two browser tabs and a live server for this test. The
      <strong>sluice</strong> replaces both with a deterministic,
      single-process sequencer and transport.
    </p>
    <p>
      The sluice runs the production runtime with the same codecs, pending
      queues, resubmit, and reconnect catch-up over an injected transport. It
      also uses the same sequencing module as the server.
      It exercises the production client paths without claiming to reproduce
      floodgate’s storage, authentication, or network boundary.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Control delivery</h2>
    <p>
      The sluice delivers data only when the test requests it. It processes and
      sequences client submissions synchronously, then keeps the resulting
      inbound frames in a queue.
      <code>settle</code> drains those frames to quiescence, while
      <code>step</code> delivers one queued frame. This separates server order
      from the order in which each replica observes it.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">test/convergence_test.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={test} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Control concurrent delivery</h2>
    <p>
      When you need to inspect an intermediate state,
      <code>step</code> advances one inbound frame at a time. It does not
      reorder operations that are already sequenced. Submission call order
      determines that order in sluice:
    </p>
    <p>
      Identify the concurrent behavior that the application requires and test
      that behavior. A general two-client test can pass while a required case
      fails. Use live smoke tests for network and browser behavior that the
      sluice does not reproduce.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={race} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Pause and resume delivery.</strong> Both <code>sluice</code> and
        <code>sluice_js</code> provide <code>pause</code> and
        <code>resume</code> to hold one client’s inbound frames.
        <code>advance(ms)</code> moves the sequencer’s logical clock for frame
        timestamps. Presence uses the target runtime’s wall clock. JavaScript
        also exposes <code>step_info</code> with delivery metadata for
        visualizations and scripted assertions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Complete the application</h2>
    <p>
      Connect a document, define a typed schema, select a merge model for each
      slot, add ripples for temporary signals, connect it to Lustre, and add
      deterministic convergence tests. Flowboard follows the same process as a
      production watershed application.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/examples">example catalog</a> applies this process to
      thirteen browser applications. Each entry lists its structures,
      key scenario, and run guide.
    </p>
    <p>
      The{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/tree/main/examples">
      examples directory</a> contains the source files.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">You can now</span>
    <p>
      Write multi-client convergence tests that run under <code>gleam test</code>
      without external infrastructure. The tests use deterministic delivery and
      the same client code that runs in production.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
